<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.12.3: Graphing Solutions and Interpreting Points</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00588</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.12.3: Graphing Solutions and Interpreting Points</md:title>
    <md:uuid>52fdbb22-1f3f-4485-8019-fc1c9058a6f9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>For questions 1 - 7, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>A vendor at the Saturday Market makes $9 profit on each necklace she sells and $5 profit on each bracelet. Find a combination of necklaces and bracelets that she could sell to make the profit listed in each question.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--q1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Exactly $100 profit.
     </para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 10 necklaces and 2 bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Tq2 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>More than $100 profit.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. 0 necklaces and 21 bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--q3 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an equation whose solution is the combination of necklaces \((n)\) and bracelets \((b)\) she could sell and make exactly $100 profit.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \(9n+5b=100\), where \(n\) represents the number of necklaces sold and \(b\) represents the number of bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--q4 -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality whose solutions are the combinations of necklaces \((n)\) and bracelets \((b)\) she could sell and make more than $100 profit.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \(9n+5b&gt;100\) where \(n\) represents the number of necklaces sold and \(b\) represents the number of bracelets.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course.Graph the linear inequality \(y&gt;\frac52x−4\) using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--START QUESTION 5 -->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <!--REPLACE IMAGE BELOW WITH CORRECT IMAGE-->
            <figure>
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality. Number of bracelets. Number of necklaces.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_06_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_0ace0214-876b-4331-8699-f38ca15a2ec8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is (3,18.6) a solution to the inequality?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Both.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Both.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END ACTIVITY YES/NO DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <list>
            <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para>The answer is both because:</para>
            <item>Yes, it is a solution. The point (3,18.6) is in the shaded region.</item>
            <item>No, it is not a solution. The vendor can't sell 18.6 bracelets.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
  
  </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <exercise data-wait-for-event="eventShowMore1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Write an inequality using two variables \(x\) and \(y\) where the solution would be represented by shading the entire coordinate plane.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
  
    <para>\(x^2+y^2\geq0\)</para>
  
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise data-wait-for-event="eventShowMore1">
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality using two variables \(x\) and \(y\) where the solution would be represented by not shading any of the coordinate plane.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
  
    
    
    <para>\(x^2+y^2&lt;0\)</para>
  
  </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
